From: cyy <cyyever@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Jul 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] kernelbase: append --no-sandbox --in-process-gpu to steamwebhelper.exe

Steam's CEF host steamwebhelper.exe crashes under Wine on macOS without two
Chromium flags:

  - --no-sandbox: CEF's sandbox hooks the NT kernel and, under Wine's
    incomplete sandbox emulation, deadlocks its own cross-thread IPC
    (src/common/pipes.cpp "Stalled cross-thread pipe" -> Fatal assert ->
    _ExitOnFatalAssert; the host exits).

  - --in-process-gpu: the out-of-process GPU process cannot create an
    ANGLE/D3D11 window surface on the browser window under Wine+DXMT
    (eglCreateWindowSurface failed; SwapChain11.cpp), so the UI never paints.
    Running the GPU in-process sidesteps the cross-process surface.

Whisky previously injected these via an IFEO "Debugger" wrapper around
steamwebhelper.exe (steamwebhelper_wrapper.exe + a steamwebhelper_real.exe copy
+ a per-bottle registry value, on top of patch 0010's IFEO-debugger support).
That is a lot of machinery for two flags. Wine already has a
hack_append_command_line() table that appends flags per-exe (Warhammer2.exe and
SummerIslands.exe already get --in-process-gpu there), so this adds a
steamwebhelper.exe entry instead. The substring match catches every
steamwebhelper.exe launch (browser + renderer/gpu/utility children), matching
the prior wrapper's behavior, and lets us delete the wrapper binary, the _real
copy, the IFEO registry setup, and the Steam.swift plumbing for it.

---
diff --git a/dlls/kernelbase/process.c b/dlls/kernelbase/process.c
--- a/dlls/kernelbase/process.c
+++ b/dlls/kernelbase/process.c
@@ -617,6 +617,7 @@ static const WCHAR *hack_append_command_line( const WCHAR *cmd )
         {L"Red Tie Runner.exe", L" --use-angle=gl"},
         {L"UnrealCEFSubProcess.exe", L" --use-gl=swiftshader", "2316580"},
         {L"UnrealCEFSubProcess.exe", L" --use-angle=d3d9", "2684500"},
+        {L"steamwebhelper.exe", L" --no-sandbox --in-process-gpu"},
     };
     unsigned int i;
     char sgi[64];
